<a href="http://gangrey.com/1428">
<h2 class="design">Digging</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">YOUR OWN, OR SOMEONE ELSE'S?</h4>

<p>

Every month at the St. Petersburg Times, three stories are chosen to win prizes. The judges are the winners from the previous three months. The categories are Deadline, Non-Deadline and Investigative. The last one tends to generate especially vigorous discussion.<br />
<br />
Here's the problem. Everyone seems to have a slightly different definition of investigative reporting. Must it expose previously hidden wrongdoing on the part of the powerful? Is it enough to disclose a previously unreported investigation that someone else conducted? What threshold must be crossed before a story can truly be called investigative?<br />
<br />
Does it have to result in indictments?<br />
<br />
To investigate, according to the Random House unabridged dictionary, is to "examine, study, or inquire into systematically; search or examine into the particulars of; examine in detail."<br />
<br />
Isn't this what we're supposed do with most stories?
