&#8220;He knew how to instill excitement in the craft of nonfiction writing; how to make what happened in, say, the Lower Arctic section of West Warwick seem as relevant and as dynamic as anything occurring in Manhattan or in Boston,&#8221; said Dan Barry, who was a member of The Journal&#8217;s 1994 Pulitzer-winning team and is now with The New York Times. &#8220;I wrote a magazine piece once, about a particularly troubling arson case in Providence, and he sent me an encouraging note. The note pretty much changed my life.&#8221;<br />
