<a href="http://gangrey.com/1594">
<h2 class="design">The Questions</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">help a brother out</h4>

<p>

I'm entertaining a few questions from some of our colleagues in Germany for a cool j-outfit called ReporterForum. Rather than answer these myself, I'd love to open them up to everybody. If you have a minute, and have a thought on one or all of these, answer in the comments. It'll be fun.<br />
<br />
Thanks.<br />
<br />
Here goes:<br />
<br />
The discussions on gangrey.com are often fascinating case studies in writing decision making - What are the most discussed topics in the threads? <br />
<br />
Which entry entailed the most controversial discussion?<br />
<br />
The mission of your blog is "prolonging the slow dead of newspapers&#8220; &#8211; sounds not very optimistic? <br />
<br />
How would you describe the current situation of the press in the US? <br />
<br />
What does "narrative journalism" mean to you? <br />
<br />
The main competence of a writer is credibility &#8211; how do you explain the lack of credibilty journalism suffers in these days? What can we do to improve it?  <br />
<br />
What are the main skills a writer should have? <br />
<br />
In the storytelling manifesto you're saying: "If we intend to have jobs 20 years from now, if we intend to own any validity in our fight for progress and reform, we have to reverse the trends that infect our business." What are these destructive trends in your opinion? How can we fight for progress?<br />
<br />
How can a blog like gangrey contribute to a new awarness of narrative journalism?
